ory about a group of  kidnapped scientists being forced to  build a time machine the storyline was  finally changed focus on the shadowy  agency agent Gabriel Logan and his  partner as they investigate an emerging  bioterrorist threat in the form of the  Syphon Filter virus much of the story  was delivered in game in the form of  radio communications with brief between  mission cutscenes serving to highlight  the bigger more cinematic moments it  seems Sony's faith was well placed as  Syphon Filter was released in 1999 to  critical acclaim giant bombs then  GameSpot's Jeff Gerstmann said though  the game does have some minor problems  the overall experience is nothing short  of outstanding the game was praised in  particular for its AI with commendations  for how certain enemies would react to  the death of a comrade and how enemy  behaviour would change each time a level  was played sometimes in the form of  taking cover or carrying different  weapons one particular standout weapon  was the Taser when fired at an enemy the  camera would reposition to focus on the  victim as they arrived in agony and if  the trigger was held long enough they'd  eventually burst into flames  Syphon Filter exceeded the expectations  of both stone  and eidetic selling over 1 million units  in its first year and work immediately  began on a sequel released in 2000  Syphon Filter 2 saw the first game story  expanded with liberal use of between  mission cutscenes causing the game to be  released across two discs but the  sequels biggest change was the inclusion  of two players with screen deathmatch  while rudimentary by today's standards  it stood out at release thanks to  brilliant level design including secret  areas to get the drop on your opponent  and impressive arsenal of weapons and a  satisfying shooting and movement from  the single-player experience  shortly after Syphon Filter 2 was  released Sony acquired eidetic and the  company was renamed bench studios the  newly renamed s